# Playtest inbox

Friends Send from the itch playtest build. Those notes become GitHub Issues labeled `playtest` on `Nathan-Elequin/swipe`, through the Cloudflare Worker. That list is the inbox. Do not ask Nathan to paste the notes.

This rule has the same weight as `content-board.mdc` (gold boxes). Do not skip it because the chat is about UI, keys, art, a different card, or process.

Inbox source: Issues created by the playtest build's feedback paths (right-click comment, F2 Feedback, tier list Send). In practice that is label `playtest` on `Nathan-Elequin/swipe`.

This rule fires only in this repo.

Public playtest: https://nathan-elequin.itch.io/spinheritance-playtest (`playtest-ship.mdc`). Local F5 is the same game on this PC. It does **not** count as deployed.

## Every chat in this repo

Before the requested work, do **both** (order between them does not matter):

1. Gold boxes (`content-board.mdc`).
2. Issues with label `playtest` (`gh issue list --label playtest`, open and recently closed, or GitHub MCP).

Keep tracking **every still-open Issue** in every reply, including ones already summarized, until it belongs in **Closed issues** (below) or Nathan **verbally describes that ticket as closed**. Then close it on GitHub in that same turn. Do not hide, table, or drop an open Issue because he replied about something else. A finished local fix is not Closed until the itch playtest has it — so push that build in the same turn (`playtest-ship.mdc`).

Linear bugs (one reading, no new rule, no number change): start the fix in that same turn, export **Web Playtest**, `butler push` to `nathan-elequin/spinheritance-playtest` (`playtest-ship.mdc`), then close the Issue and put it in **Closed issues**. Do not wait for him to say ship. Crashes / cannot-play: same auto-fix.

If a report has two readings that would change the code, Paper Fight. Ask. Do not patch it as a bug.

Balance / “this felt strong”: list it. Do not change numbers or rules unless he says so in that chat.

Empty open list: **Open issues** still appears, with one line that none are open.

Empty closed list: **Closed issues** still appears, with one line that none are new to show. That includes the case where every live-on-itch close has already been listed in a chat Nathan saw.

In a handoff task (`design/handoff/<task-id>/TASK.md`): only auto-fix if the files are on that task's "Files you may touch" list. Otherwise still list the Issues in **Open issues**.

## Reply shape in this repo

After the headline, use Next steps (if any), then Notes (if any), then **Open issues**, then **Closed issues**. Those last two are equal sections. Neither is Next steps. Neither is Notes. Friend-voice's two-section default still applies above them; this repo always adds these two sections at the end, in that order.

### ⬜ Open issues

GitHub Issues labeled `playtest` that are still open **and** whose fix is not yet playable on the itch playtest.

Categorize them into whichever of these buckets have items (omit empty buckets):

1. **Overall thoughts and core gameplay** — including Challenge, Adventure, the map, and the cabin.
2. **Confusion** — uncertainty, wrong or missing information, inconsistency.
3. **Bugs and visual glitches**
4. **Crashes and cannot-play**
5. **Art and music** — do not draw or replace PNG bytes (`unique-art.mdc`). ART-REQUEST. Do not invent music files unless Nathan asked.
6. **Playtest tooling** — hub, Feedback, tier list, Send, right-click comment.
7. **Content** — item and monster balance, numbered card strength.

Each open item:

- Starts with ⬜
- Issue number, one short line of what they reported, link

Example: `1. ⬜ [#22](url) — Unplayable items (Sledgehammer) should show a grey overlay on the hotbar.`

### ✅ Closed issues

Only Issues whose fix you are **confident** is fully deployed: merged, and playable on https://nathan-elequin.itch.io/spinheritance-playtest. Local code, F5, or an unpushed commit is not enough.

Each closed item:

- Starts with ✅
- **Bold short phrase** naming the change (not a restatement of the report)
- Then an em dash and one sentence of what you did
- Include the Issue number and link somewhere in that line

Example: `1. ✅ **Unplayable hatch** — Hotbar cards you cannot use get a stripe overlay you can still read through ([#22](url)).`

When that itch bar is met in a chat, close the GitHub Issue in that same turn if it is still open, and put it here instead of Open issues. **Closed issues in the reply and closing GitHub are the same action.** If it is in this section, GitHub must already be closed, or you close it before you send the reply.

List a Closed issue **once**. After it has appeared under **Closed issues** in a chat message Nathan saw (this chat or an earlier one), do not list it again in later replies or later chats. Open issues still stay in **Open issues** every reply until they qualify for this section. Worked example: [#22](https://github.com/Nathan-Elequin/swipe/issues/22) was shown as closed; do not bring it up again.

## Close

Close a GitHub Issue when **either**:

1. Nathan clearly says that ticket is closed / done / finished / live on itch, or
2. The fix is playable on the itch playtest (the bar for **Closed issues**).

Close it yourself in that turn. Do not wait for him to confirm.

How: Shell `gh issue close <n> --repo Nathan-Elequin/swipe`. Do **not** use GitHub MCP `issue_write` (or any MCP that pops a Submit / review card) for this close. Nathan must never open GitHub, click Submit, or approve a close form.

If Auto-review blocks `gh`, retry that same command with `request_smart_mode_approval`. Do not hand him a GitHub click instead.

Do not invent copy to “fix” confusion unless he gave the words. Jam owns `data/player-copy.json`. Unique art still applies.

## Do not

- Do not skip this read because the chat is about something else.
- Do not ask Nathan to paste playtest notes.
- Do not stop listing an open Issue until it is live on the itch playtest, or he says to close it.
- Do not move an Issue into **Closed issues** because the code exists locally, or because F5 works on this PC.
- Do not list an Issue under **Closed issues** while it is still open on GitHub.
- Do not ask Nathan to close, Submit, or confirm a GitHub Issue. That is your job.
- Do not close a GitHub Issue because he ignored a summary.
- Do not change balance numbers from Content reports unless he says so in that chat.
- Do not treat a gray bug/rule report as a linear bug.
- Do not re-list a Closed issue in a later reply or later chat after it has already appeared under **Closed issues** in a message Nathan saw.
